; =============================================================================
; Tunah tuner -- data flow optimization
; =============================================================================
; Purpose       : Remove merge instructions whose incoming values are already
;                the same value. A merge that receives one value on every edge
;                computes that value, and an identical load on both edges is a
;                single load, which is the load forwarding that data flow
;                analysis exists to expose.
; Stage         : Instruction level machine IL, after the merge points of a
;                region are known but before instruction selection.
; Vocabulary    : phi, sel and the loads from the shared directory vocabulary.
;                ite is deliberately absent: a predicated select is a control
;                flow operator and belongs to ctrlflow-opt.tuner, and mov/copy
;                elimination belongs to reduntant-instr-elim.tuner. No rule
;                body is shared between those files and this one.
; Preconditions : phi takes the value of whichever edge was taken, so phi of
;                one value is that value. sel(a, b, c) yields a when c is
;                non-zero. Loads are not speculatable here: the rule below
;                removes a load only when both edges load the same address in
;                the same width, so the two loads are the same value and at
;                most one of them can be live. Loads with side effects, traps
;                or ordering constraints must not be represented as loadN; the
;                adapter has to reject them before ingestion.
; Cost model    : A merge costs 3 and a load costs 2, so both rules are
;                cost-reducing and win extraction.
; Termination   : Every rule deletes a merge node, and no rule creates a merge,
;                so each matched subtree strictly shrinks. The nested select
;                rule consumes one select and produces one, but it also
;                removes the duplicated condition operand, so the matched
;                subterm strictly decreases and the set is monotone.
;
; Operator signatures required by this file:
; signatures: phi 2, sel 3, load32 1, load64 1
; =============================================================================

; A merge that receives the same value on both edges computes that value.
(rule df-phi-same-arms (phi ?a ?a) ?a)
; Two loads of the same address and width in the same width are one value, so
; the merge above them is that load. This is redundant load elimination across
; an edge pair.
(rule df-phi-same-load32 (phi (load32 ?p) (load32 ?p)) (load32 ?p))
; Same, for the double width load.
(rule df-phi-same-load64 (phi (load64 ?p) (load64 ?p)) (load64 ?p))
; A select whose arms agree is that value, whatever the condition.
(rule df-sel-same-arms (sel ?a ?a ?c) ?a)
; Two loads of the same address and width under a select are one value, so
; the select is that load.
(rule df-sel-same-load32 (sel (load32 ?p) (load32 ?p) ?c) (load32 ?p))
; Same, for the double width load.
(rule df-sel-same-load64 (sel (load64 ?p) (load64 ?p) ?c) (load64 ?p))
; A select nested immediately below a select with the same condition and the
; same false arm is redundant: the inner select already returns ?a when ?c is
; non-zero and ?b when it is not, which is what the outer one returns. The
; condition is evaluated once instead of twice, and one select is deleted.
(rule df-sel-nested (sel (sel ?a ?b ?c) ?b ?c) (sel ?a ?b ?c))
